#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167900} :activity/id #uuid "eed4b5fc-95ba-5979-9241-b92cdec87053" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168037 :tutorial/content "In a homogeneous boundary value problem (BVP) that depends on a parameter $\\lambda$, most values of $\\lambda$ lead only to the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$.  \n\nThe special values of $\\lambda$ that produce *nontrivial* solutions are called **eigenvalues**, and the corresponding nontrivial solutions are called **eigenfunctions**.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad 4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nLet's find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. \n\nThe method for solving the differential equation depends on whether $\\lambda = 0$ or $\\lambda \\neq 0.$ To find the eigenvalues, we must therefore investigate these two possibilities as separate cases.\n\nOn the next slide, we will analyze the case where $\\lambda = 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1378993d-8bac-50d2-b1ea-4f7c3a1f5ec2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14646 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "887046eb-da38-509e-bc00-d45adf4aa497" :step/math-academy-id 592744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166566 :question/id #uuid "877e1518-96ed-5b31-9e05-22990ce1cb72" :question/math-academy-id "e-21808"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252}] :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "4251a9bf-5cd3-5cfc-ae56-c1aa466b5998" :step/math-academy-id 592745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166565 :question/id #uuid "738bd502-dec6-589b-b198-5040fe7e3906" :question/math-academy-id "e-21806"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049937}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397263}] :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "dc1ec685-f38d-51a2-9240-4f3a1f1346e1" :step/math-academy-id 592748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168038 :tutorial/content "When the auxiliary equation has purely imaginary roots, the solutions are trigonometric, and the boundary conditions usually force $\\lambda$ to take on a discrete set of values.\nSo, we again look for values of $\\lambda$ that allow a nontrivial solution.\n\nWe will now find the **eigenvalues** and **eigenfunctions** for the boundary value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda^2 y = 0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0, \\quad y(4) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe consider two cases based on the value of $\\lambda,$ since they correspond to different forms of the general solution.\n\n**Case 1: $\\lambda = 0$**\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation $y'' = 0.$\nIntegrating this equation twice gives the general solution:\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nTo apply the boundary conditions, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$\n\n- Applying $y'(0) = 0$ gives $A = 0.$ The solution becomes $y = B.$\n\n- Applying $y(4) = 0$ then gives $B = 0.$\n\nThis yields the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Since an eigenfunction must be nontrivial, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2: $\\lambda > 0$**\n\nThe auxiliary equation is $m^2 + 9\\lambda^2 = 0,$ which has roots $m = \\pm 3i\\lambda.$\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x) + B\\sin (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. First, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny' = -3A\\lambda\\sin(3\\lambda x) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Applying the condition $y'(0) = 0$ yields:\n\n$$\n-3A\\lambda\\sin(0) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(0) = 0 \\quad\\implies\\quad 3B\\lambda = 0.\n$$\n\nSince we are in the case where $\\lambda > 0,$ this requires $B = 0.$ The solution simplifies to:\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Next, applying the condition $y(4) = 0$ yields:\n \n\n$$\nA\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n For a nontrivial solution, we must have $A \\neq 0,$ which means we require\n \n\n$$\n\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n This condition is met when $12\\lambda$ is an odd multiple of $\\pi/2:$\n\n$$\n12\\lambda = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\lambda$ gives the set of **eigenvalues**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **eigenfunctions** are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = A_n \\cos(3\\lambda_n x) , \\quad A_n \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e52b01a-4f53-5d27-9984-dd731308d87d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14650 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "3e7ba88a-596c-53db-b70b-6b89ee975318" :step/math-academy-id 592751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168055 :tutorial/content "Next, we consider the case $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions of \n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,\n$$\n\nfor $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a repeated root $m = 9\\lambda.$ The general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Applying the first boundary condition, $y(0) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\ny(0) = Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution simplifies to $y = Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.$ Its derivative is\n\n$$\ny' = Be^{9\\lambda x} + 9B\\lambda xe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\n- Applying the second boundary condition, $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(3B{e}^{27\\lambda })+5(B{e}^{27\\lambda }+27B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 12B{e}^{27\\lambda }+5B{e}^{27\\lambda }+135B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{27\\lambda }(17+135\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a nontrivial solution (an eigenfunction), we require $B\\neq 0.$ Since $e^{27\\lambda}$ is never zero, we must have\n\n$$\n17+135\\lambda = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad \\lambda = -\\dfrac{17}{135}.\n$$\n\nThis is our **eigenvalue**. The corresponding **eigenfunction** is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Bxe^{9(-17/135) x} = Bxe^{-17x/15}.\n$$\n\nNote that any nonzero constant multiple of this function is also an eigenfunction." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4d9fb35-7991-5b49-bfd6-c82ad2ef1c69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14892 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem"} :step/id #uuid "81f0071e-ac02-5b8f-ba91-88209136e903" :step/math-academy-id 608067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168056 :tutorial/content "We begin by considering  the case $\\lambda = 0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation, $y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,$ gives a much simpler equation:\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0,$ implies:\n\n$$\ny(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0.\n$$\n\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax.$\n\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$ Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(A\\cdot 3)+5(A) & =0 \\\\ 12A+5A & =0 \\\\ 17A & =0\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0,$ we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue. \n\nNext, we will check the case where $\\lambda \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b3458843-3f77-548b-b991-69c3d163d75d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14893 :tutorial/title "Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem"} :step/id #uuid "d95ffe14-fb14-518a-ac26-6970a9d57acf" :step/math-academy-id 608068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167958}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166995}]}
